  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` for the blizzard mountain pass. It typechecks, but I couldn't build or run it, so I haven't seen a rendered frame. Light levels, fog density and the flakes' visual weight are untested guesses.\n\n- **Terrain:** a procedural V-shaped valley, about 4 m wide at the bottom, that follows a winding trail and climbs gradually. Snow shades from white to blue-grey, with dark rock on steep or noisy areas. A narrow, darker trail strip runs up the middle. Boulders and spires, each with a snow cap, are scattered on the slopes.\n- **Trail and cabin:** 18 wooden poles, each with a crossbar and snow cap, line the trail. A wooden cabin with a gabled, snow-capped roof sits halfway up. Its window uses a warm emissive material, with a warm point light beside it.\n- **Snow:** two flake sizes, 5200 small and 1700 large. They are streaked boxes angled along the wind and always cover the area around the camera. Between 3500 and 6900 are visible at once, so the count never drops below the required 1500. A periodic gust adds flakes and makes them longer and faster. It also thickens the fog and strengthens the camera sway.\n- **Sky and fog:** a flat pale-grey overcast sky, with exponential fog in the same colour so far peaks fade to soft silhouettes.\n- **Camera:** it walks the trail at 1.7 m eye height with sway, bob and slight roll, using `app.camera.setPose`. Each 64 s loop ends with a brief fog whiteout that hides the cut back to the start.\n- **Contract:** it draws into `#app` and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after `app.ready()`.\n\nThe camera relies on `app.camera` existing at runtime. In the engine source its flag is default-on, so it should work, but I couldn't confirm it. If it's undefined, the camera would stay at the start.\n\n",
